Task 1: Attach Additional Storage to an Existing VM
Subtask 1.1: Verify Current Storage Configuration
First, let's examine the current storage setup of our target virtual machine.

Access the web console and navigate to the virtual machines section
Select your target VM from the list of available machines
Check current disk configuration by viewing the storage tab
From the command line on the host system, verify the VM's current storage:

# List all virtual machines and their storage
virsh list --all

# Get detailed information about VM storage
virsh domblklist your-vm-name

# Check disk usage within the VM
virsh console your-vm-name
Once connected to the VM console:

# Display current disk partitions
lsblk

# Show filesystem usage
df -h

# List all block devices
fdisk -l
Subtask 1.2: Create Additional Virtual Disk
Now we'll create a new virtual disk file that can be attached to our VM.

Navigate to the storage pool in your web console
Create a new volume with the following specifications:
Name: additional-disk-01
Format: qcow2
Size: 5 GB
Alternatively, create the disk using command line:

# Create a new qcow2 disk image
qemu-img create -f qcow2 /var/lib/libvirt/images/additional-disk-01.qcow2 5G

# Verify the disk creation
qemu-img info /var/lib/libvirt/images/additional-disk-01.qcow2

# Set appropriate permissions
chown qemu:qemu /var/lib/libvirt/images/additional-disk-01.qcow2
chmod 644 /var/lib/libvirt/images/additional-disk-01.qcow2
Subtask 1.3: Attach Disk to Running VM
With the disk created, we'll now attach it to our running virtual machine.

Using Web Console:

Select your VM from the virtual machines list
Navigate to the Storage tab
Click Add Disk button
Configure the new disk:
Source: Select the disk we created
Device: vdb (or next available)
Bus: virtio
Cache: default
Apply changes
Using Command Line:

# Attach disk to running VM (hot-plug)
virsh attach-disk your-vm-name \
    /var/lib/libvirt/images/additional-disk-01.qcow2 \
    vdb \
    --driver qemu \
    --subdriver qcow2 \
    --type disk \
    --mode rw

# Verify attachment
virsh domblklist your-vm-name
Subtask 1.4: Configure the New Disk Inside VM
Now we need to configure the newly attached disk within the virtual machine.

Connect to your VM console
Verify the new disk is detected:
# Check if new disk is visible
lsblk

# Verify new disk device
fdisk -l | grep /dev/vdb
Create a partition on the new disk:
# Start fdisk for the new disk
sudo fdisk /dev/vdb

# Inside fdisk, execute these commands:
# n (new partition)
# p (primary partition)
# 1 (partition number)
# [Enter] (default first sector)
# [Enter] (default last sector)
# w (write changes)
Format the new partition:
# Create ext4 filesystem
sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/vdb1

# Verify filesystem creation
sudo blkid /dev/vdb1
Mount the new disk:
# Create mount point
sudo mkdir /mnt/additional-storage

# Mount the partition
sudo mount /dev/vdb1 /mnt/additional-storage

# Verify mount
df -h | grep additional-storage
Make mount persistent:
# Get UUID of the partition
sudo blkid /dev/vdb1

# Add entry to fstab
echo "UUID=your-uuid-here /mnt/additional-storage ext4 defaults 0 2" | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab

# Test fstab entry
sudo umount /mnt/additional-storage
sudo mount -a
df -h | grep additional-storage
Task 2: Detach and Remove Storage from a VM
Subtask 2.1: Prepare for Storage Removal
Before removing storage, we must ensure data safety and proper unmounting.

Check what's using the storage:
# Check for open files on the mount point
sudo lsof /mnt/additional-storage

# Check for processes using the mount point
sudo fuser -v /mnt/additional-storage
Backup important data (if any):
# Create test data first
sudo touch /mnt/additional-storage/test-file.txt
echo "Test data for storage lab" | sudo tee /mnt/additional-storage/test-file.txt

# Backup the data
sudo cp -r /mnt/additional-storage/* /tmp/storage-backup/
Subtask 2.2: Safely Unmount the Storage
Proper unmounting is crucial before detaching storage from a VM.

Unmount the filesystem:
# Unmount the additional storage
sudo umount /mnt/additional-storage

# Verify unmount
df -h | grep additional-storage
mount | grep vdb1
Remove from fstab:
# Edit fstab to remove the entry
sudo sed -i '/additional-storage/d' /etc/fstab

# Verify removal
cat /etc/fstab | grep additional-storage
Subtask 2.3: Detach Disk from VM
Now we can safely detach the disk from the virtual machine.

Using Web Console:

Navigate to your VM's storage tab
Select the additional disk (vdb)
Click the detach/remove button
Confirm the removal
Using Command Line:

# Detach disk from running VM (hot-unplug)
virsh detach-disk your-vm-name vdb

# Verify detachment
virsh domblklist your-vm-name

# Check inside VM that disk is gone
lsblk
Subtask 2.4: Remove Disk File (Optional)
If you no longer need the disk file, you can remove it completely.

# Remove the disk image file
rm /var/lib/libvirt/images/additional-disk-01.qcow2

# Verify removal
ls -la /var/lib/libvirt/images/ | grep additional-disk
Task 3: Verify Storage Operations Using Web Console
Subtask 3.1: Monitor Storage Changes in Real-Time
The web console provides excellent visibility into storage operations.

Access the main dashboard of your virtualization platform
Navigate to Storage Pools section
Review storage utilization and available space
Check storage pool health and status
Subtask 3.2: Verify VM Storage Configuration
Select your test VM from the virtual machines list
Review the Storage tab to confirm:
Original system disk is present
Additional disk has been removed
No orphaned storage references exist
Subtask 3.3: Check Storage Events and Logs
Navigate to the Events/Logs section
Filter for storage-related events
Verify successful attach/detach operations
From command line, check system logs:

# Check libvirt logs for storage operations
sudo journalctl -u libvirtd | grep -i storage

# Check for any storage-related errors
sudo journalctl | grep -i "disk\|storage" | tail -20

# Verify VM configuration
virsh dumpxml your-vm-name | grep -A 5 -B 5 disk
Subtask 3.4: Performance and Health Verification
Monitor VM performance after storage changes
Check disk I/O statistics:
# Monitor disk I/O
iostat -x 1 5

# Check VM disk performance
virsh domstats your-vm-name | grep block
Verify filesystem integrity:
# Check remaining filesystem
sudo fsck /dev/vda1

# Verify system disk health
sudo smartctl -a /dev/vda
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Disk Attachment Fails
Symptoms: Error messages when trying to attach disk

Solutions:

# Check if VM is running
virsh list | grep your-vm-name

# Verify disk file permissions
ls -la /var/lib/libvirt/images/additional-disk-01.qcow2

# Check available device names
virsh domblklist your-vm-name
Issue 2: Cannot Unmount Filesystem
Symptoms: "Device busy" error when unmounting

Solutions:

# Find processes using the mount point
sudo fuser -km /mnt/additional-storage

# Force unmount (use with caution)
sudo umount -f /mnt/additional-storage

# Lazy unmount as last resort
sudo umount -l /mnt/additional-storage
Issue 3: Disk Not Visible in VM
Symptoms: Attached disk doesn't appear in VM

Solutions:

# Rescan SCSI bus
echo "- - -" | sudo tee /sys/class/scsi_host/host*/scan

# Check dmesg for disk detection
dmesg | grep -i disk

# Verify virtio modules are loaded
lsmod | grep virtio
Best Practices for VM Storage Management
Storage Planning
Always plan storage requirements before VM deployment
Use appropriate disk formats (qcow2 for flexibility, raw for performance)
Implement proper backup strategies before making changes
Monitor storage performance regularly
Safety Procedures
Always unmount filesystems before detaching disks
Backup critical data before storage operations
Test storage changes in non-production environments first
Document storage configurations for future reference
Performance Optimization
Use virtio drivers for better performance
Choose appropriate cache modes based on use case
Monitor disk I/O patterns and adjust accordingly
Consider storage pool placement for optimal performance
